<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moruroa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mururoa, Mururoa Atoll</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moruroa, also spelled Mururoa, is an atoll in the Tuamotu archipelago of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conducted most of its nuclear weapons tests there between 1966 and 1996, in the atmosphere until 1974 and then underground, together with nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fangataufa">Fangataufa</a>. The tests provoked protests across the Pacific, notably in 1995, and their health and environmental effects remain contested.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moruroa.jpg" alt="Moruroa" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moruroa_atoll.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Moruroa, also spelled Mururoa, is an atoll in the Tuamotu Archipelago of French Polynesia, about 1,250 kilometres south-east of Tahiti, and is part of the commune of Tureia. The first recorded visit was by Commander Philip Carteret on HMS Swallow in 1767, who named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Osnaburgh Island. The British whaler Matilda was wrecked there in 1792. The atoll was not continuously inhabited: Frederick William Beechey found it empty in 1826, and a visiting ship in 1834 killed all but three of the inhabitants it found. Copra workers lived there briefly in the late nineteenth century and again in 1942 to 1943 and 1950 to 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> established the atoll as a nuclear test site on 21 September 1962, and construction began in May 1963, with the atoll of Hao as a support base. The first test, Aldebaran, a plutonium fission bomb exploded in the lagoon, took place on 2 July 1966 despite objections from about 30 members of the Polynesian Territorial Assembly. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> was present for a test on 10 September 1966. Forty-one atmospheric tests were conducted between 1966 and 1974, after which France moved underground amid intense world pressure, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> contributed by sending two frigates in July 1973.</p><p>The underground tests cracked the atoll and raised fears that radioactive material would escape. On 25 July 1979 a device stuck halfway down its 800-metre shaft was detonated at half the usual depth, causing a large submarine landslide, a tsunami and a crack two kilometres long. New tests on 5 September and 2 October 1995 caused worldwide protests, an embargo of French wine and riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. The last test at Moruroa took place on 27 December 1995, and the site was dismantled afterwards. In all 181 explosions took place there, though nuclear scientists working at the site claim 175.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The atoll has had no permanent inhabitants since 1952. In February 2021 the French National Institute of Health and Medical Research published a report on the health consequences of the atmospheric tests in French Polynesia for the general population and for civilian and military workers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moruroa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Norman Kirk's Labour government sent naval frigates in 1973 to protest against French nuclear tests at <b>Mururoa Atoll</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1966 to 1996 France carried out 193 nuclear tests at <b>Moruroa</b> and Fangataufa atolls, first in the atmosphere and later underground, bringing money and jobs but lasting health and environmental harm.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-polynesia">French Polynesia</a></li></ul></section>
